for hundreds of years  Muslims used to go out into empty fields  looking at the sky to figure out what  Ramadan begins these days though clerics  here in Pakistan and elsewhere use  telescopes to get first sight of the new  moon once spotted Ramadan starts it's  the holiest month in the Islamic  calendar when Muslims stop eating and  drinking from sunrise to sunset the  fasting allows worshipers to devote  themselves to their faith and remember  the sufferings of the poor but during  the unprecedented coronavirus emergency  Muslims are abandoning many of Ramadan's  traditions and rituals they won't be  able to invite their loved ones the  neighbors or the poor to join the  evening fast breaking meal known as  iftar as ghovat 19 spreads restrictions  are being enforced governments have  closed borders impose curfews lock downs  and encourage social distancing to  contain the contagion Islam's holiest  site in Mecca is usually teeming with  worshipers from all over the world  during Ramadan it's closed this year as  part of the Santa government's campaign  to curb the virus spread getting people  everywhere to observe social distancing  in Ramadan won't be easy  the highlight of Ramadan is the night of  destiny where Muslims believe the holy  book the Quran was first revealed to the  Prophet Muhammad and where the gates of  heaven are open Muslims  spent the night in contemplation and  prayers Emo's a ritual they are being  asked to perform at home this time  Islam's most sacred month man to  encourage unity praying together and  breaking their fast together may end up  being a month of relative solitude  Hashima para al jazeera
 

